---
// The key to the board: what the score is, how far to trust the ranking, what an
// expanded case shows, and what a "possible leak" tag means. Static copy over the
// moment register in src/lib/moments.js. Collapsed by default so the board stays
// near the top of the page.
import { MOMENTS, STAGES, STAGE_ORDER } from "../lib/moments.js";

const groups = STAGE_ORDER.map((stage) => ({
  stage,
  ...STAGES[stage],
  events: Object.entries(MOMENTS).filter(([, v]) => v.stage === stage),
}));
---
<details class="key" id="reading-the-board">
  <summary>How to read this board</summary>

  <h2>The big-case score</h2>
  <p>
    Each model gives every case it predicts a score from 0 to 1 for how much the case matters: 0 is a routine
    petition, 1 is a landmark. The <strong>Average</strong> column is the mean of the models' scores, and the
    board is sorted by it. The score is a pre-registered opinion, not a forecast: it says nothing about whether the
    Court will take the case, and it is never marked right or wrong. It is graded later by a separate evaluator
    for the quality of its reasoning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Treat the order as rough. Neighbouring rows usually sit closer together than the three models sit to each
    other within a single row, so the gap between two adjacent ranks is smaller than the disagreement it is built
    from. Rows are also not strictly comparable when they answer different questions: the models tend to read
    stakes higher once a case reaches the merits than at the petition stage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where the average says "2 of 3 models", one model has not yet predicted the case's current stage; its earlier
    read is shown inside the case but not averaged in. Scored forecasts, as opposed to these opinions, live
    on the <a href="https://github.com/ModelMirrorAI/fedcourtsai/blob/main/metrics/README.md">leaderboard in the repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What an expanded case shows</h2>
  <p>
    A case can be predicted several times as it moves through the Court, and each prediction answers one question
    asked at one point in time. There are three stages, each with its own question. Under each question, the board
    lists every model's newest prediction: its big-case score, the probability it gave, and (at the first two stages)
    the single outcome it thought most likely. The probability is what gets scored, so "chance the Court takes the
    case: 12%, prediction: turn it down" is one position, not two.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Prediction made</th><th>Ledger event id</th></tr>
      </thead>
      {groups.map((g) => (
        <tbody>
          <tr class="stage"><th colspan="2">{g.question}</th></tr>
          {g.events.map(([id, v]) => (
            <tr><td>{v.when.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + v.when.slice(1)}</td><td class="mono">{id}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      ))}
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    At the petition stage, "send it back for reconsideration" (a <a href="/how-it-works#gvr">GVR</a>) and "reverse
    without hearing argument" both count as the Court taking the case. For an emergency application, a partial grant
    counts as a denial. At the merits stage there is no single most-likely outcome to show; the predicted judgment and
    vote are in the linked prediction. Terms are explained in the <a href="/how-it-works#glossary">glossary</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="possible-leak">"Possible leak"</h2>
  <p>
    Models are free to search the web when they predict, and afterwards an evaluator reads what each one looked at.
    A prediction is tagged <span class="tag warn">possible leak</span> when the evaluator found signs that the model may
    have seen how the case turned out before predicting it. The tag is a warning about that one prediction, not about
    the case. A case carries the tag when one of the predictions in its average does.
  </p>
</details>

<style>
  .key { margin: 1.2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .key > summary { cursor: pointer; padding: 0.6rem 0; font-weight: 560; }
  .key[open] { padding-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .key h2 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 1.4em 0 0.4em; }
  .key h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 0.6em; }
  .key p { max-width: var(--measure); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .key table { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .key .table-scroll table { min-width: 0; }
  .key tr.stage th { font-weight: 560; font-size: 0.86rem; color: var(--muted); border-bottom-color: var(--rule); padding-top: 0.9rem; }
  .key td.mono { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .key .tag { display: inline-block; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.7rem; border: 1px solid var(--accent); color: var(--accent); border-radius: 3px; padding: 0 0.35em; }
</style>
